                <h2>Introduction</h2>
<p>What's the worst thing that can happen when running overseas advertising? Account suspension definitely ranks in the top three. Your carefully structured campaigns, the ad creatives you've spent money testing—gone overnight.</p>
<p>This guide is specifically written for operations teams, providing a step-by-step SOP for preparing appeal materials that you can follow directly. Bookmark this article now so you have it ready when problems arise.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Confirm the Suspension Reason</h2>
<p>Before preparing any materials, you need to understand <strong>why the account was suspended</strong>. Blindly preparing documents only wastes time.</p>
<h3>Check Notifications in Your Dashboard</h3>
<p>Log in to your Google Ads dashboard and navigate to Settings → Account Status. You'll typically see a red warning indicating the suspension reason. Common reasons include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Suspicious payments</strong>: Abnormal credit card information, high payment failure rate</li>
<li><strong>Policy violations</strong>: Promoted products or creatives fall under Google-prohibited categories</li>
<li><strong>Circumvention</strong>: Attempting to manipulate ad serving or evade review</li>
<li><strong>Brand safety issues</strong>: Associated domains or websites have security problems</li>
</ul>
<h3>Check Your Email</h3>
<p>Google sends an email to the account administrator simultaneously with the suspension. This email usually includes a link to the violation details page, which is your most important reference.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Operations Tip</strong>: If you manage multiple accounts, consider setting up separate notification emails for each to avoid missing critical information.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Prepare Basic Supporting Documents</h2>
<p>Different suspension reasons require different materials, but these three items are <strong>essential for virtually all appeal scenarios</strong>:</p>
<h3>Required Documents Checklist</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Document Type</th>
<th>Specific Content</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Business License</td>
<td>Clear scan or photo of company business license</td>
<td>Must be valid; information must match account registration</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ID Documents</td>
<td>Front and back of legal representative's or account manager's ID</td>
<td>In some cases, a photo of the person holding the ID may be required</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Website Ownership Proof</td>
<td>Domain certificate, hosting bill, website backend screenshot</td>
<td>Ensure the website is accessible and pages are complete</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Additional Documents by Violation Type</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Payment issues</strong>: Corporate bank statements, payment authorization proof</li>
<li><strong>Product compliance</strong>: Product certification (CE, FCC, FDA, etc.), import permits</li>
<li><strong>Brand authorization</strong>: Trademark registration certificate, brand authorization letter</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Operations Tip</strong>: Prepare all materials before submitting your appeal to avoid rejection due to incomplete documentation, which would extend recovery time.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Write Your Appeal Email</h2>
<p>The appeal email is your only channel for communicating with Google's review team. Content quality directly affects the appeal outcome.</p>
<h3>Suggested Email Structure</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Brief introduction</strong>: Company name, account ID, type of business</li>
<li><strong>State your purpose</strong>: Clearly indicate you've noted the account suspension</li>
<li><strong>Describe corrective measures</strong>: Explain corrective actions taken or planned for the violation</li>
<li><strong>Express willingness to cooperate</strong>: Commit to strictly following Google Ads policies</li>
<li><strong>Attach supporting materials</strong>: Include a list of documents in your email</li>
</ol>
<h3>Email Template Reference</h3>
<blockquote>
<p>Dear Google Ads Review Team,</p>
<p>We are [Company Name], operating Google Ads Account ID: [Account ID], primarily promoting [business type].</p>
<p>We have received the account suspension notice. After internal review, we identified [brief description of violation, e.g., "missing website privacy policy page"]. We take this seriously and have taken the following corrective actions:</p>
<ol>
<li>[Corrective action 1]</li>
<li>[Corrective action 2]</li>
</ol>
<p>We commit to strictly adhering to Google Ads policies and avoiding similar issues in the future. Please find our business license and website ownership proof attached for your review.</p>
<p>Thank you for your attention. We look forward to your response.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Submit and Follow Up</h2>
<h3>Submission Channels</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Online submission</strong>: Submit through the "Request Review" portal in Google Ads dashboard</li>
<li><strong>Email reply</strong>: Directly reply to the suspension notification email from Google</li>
</ul>
<h3>Follow-Up Timeline</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Timeline</th>
<th>Action</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>1-3 business days after submission</td>
<td>Check for email reply confirming materials received</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3-7 business days after submission</td>
<td>If no response, send a follow-up email</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>7-14 business days after submission</td>
<td>If still no response, try contacting Google Support by phone</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Operations Tip</strong>: Do not submit multiple appeals during the review period. This may delay the review queue and extend processing time.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<p><strong>Q: Can I appeal again after rejection?</strong></p>
<p>Yes. It's generally recommended to wait 5-7 business days before submitting a new appeal, while supplementing with new evidence or more detailed corrective explanations.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Can I continue advertising during the appeal?</strong></p>
<p>No. Suspended accounts cannot run any ads until the appeal is approved. Prepare a backup account in advance to avoid business interruption.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Do appeal materials need to be in English?</strong></p>
<p>Not necessarily. Official documents like business licenses can remain in their original language, but appeal emails and explanatory documents in English will result in faster processing by the review team.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>Account suspension is frustrating, but in most cases, recovery is achievable as long as the violation is clearly understood, materials are thoroughly prepared, and the appeal is submitted with a sincere attitude.</p>
<p>The key lies in two points: <strong>First, fully understand the violation reason and ensure corrections are made. Second, submit complete materials at once to avoid back-and-forth delays.</strong></p>
<p>If you're facing special difficulties with account appeals or need assistance with multi-account risk management, feel free to contact us.</p>
